# EVERY COUPLING, BESIDE THE FUSION THAT SWALLOWED IT IF ONE DID. One row per filed coupling,
# with the composed layer holding both of its ends where such a layer exists.
#
# ⚠️ A REFERRAL AND NOT A VIOLATION -- the distinction that keeps this file honest. DETECTING
#   the structure is mechanical: two coupled layers, one composed layer, both ends inside it.
#   JUDGING whether the composer should have said so is prose, and prose is a person's job. So
#   these are handed over rather than passed or failed, and a clean run HAS rows here.
#
# ⭐⭐ WHY IT MATTERS: once two coupled layers are inside one total, their dependence stops
#    being visible from outside. Whoever reads the composed figure cannot tell that part of it
#    moves together -- and that is exactly the information a fusion is supposed to preserve.
#    The schema asks the composer to say so; nothing can make them.
#
# ⭐ THE UNABSORBED ROWS ARE THE DENOMINATOR AND THEY ARE HERE ON PURPOSE. A referral that
#   printed only its findings would be as unreadable as a rule that printed only its
#   violations: five couplings examined and two absorbed is a statement; two rows is not.
#
# ⛔⛔ CORPUS ONLY, AND IT WAS THE ONE REPORT IN THIS DIRECTORY THAT FORGOT TO SAY SO. A
#    referral hands a document to a PERSON to judge, and a fixture's prose is stipulated
#    rather than authored -- there is nobody to refer it to and nothing for them to weigh.
#    No fixture files a coupling today, so this join changes no row; that is exactly the
#    moment to write it, rather than the run after the first one does.
-- pm:Coupling against asrt:Fusion/asrt:Part pairs that contain both of its ends, over the corpus.
SELECT c.filing,
       c.from_layer || ' -> ' || c.to_layer AS coupling,
       CASE WHEN a.composition IS NULL THEN '(no fusion holds both ends)'
            ELSE a.composition || '/' || a.composed_layer END AS absorbed_into,
       left(regexp_replace(c.observation, '\s+', ' ', 'g'), 56) || '...' AS what_was_observed
FROM (
    :compose(entries/couplings.sqlc)
) c
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT DISTINCT sp.part_filing AS filing, sp.from_layer, sp.to_layer,
           sp.composition, sp.composed_layer
    FROM (
        :compose(composition/sibling_parts.sqlc)
    ) sp
) a USING (filing, from_layer, to_layer)
JOIN      (
    :compose(scope/corpus.sqlc)
) sc USING (filing)
ORDER BY 1, 2
